Today — Async JS
This is the last major JS concept before React. And it's critical — every API call, every database query, every real-world operation is async.

Why Async Exists — Under the Hood
JavaScript is single threaded. One thing at a time. If fetching data took 3 seconds and JS just waited — your entire page would freeze for 3 seconds. Nothing could click, scroll, or move.
So JS says: "Start this task, move on, come back when it's done."
That's async. The mechanism that makes this work is called the Event Loop — but you don't need to master that today. Just understand the concept.

Promises — what they are
A Promise is JS saying: "I don't have this value yet, but I promise I'll give it to you when it's ready."
A Promise has 3 states:

Pending — still waiting
Resolved — got the value
Rejected — something went wrong

Example of a Promise-based wait function
In `day3.js`, the first exercise built `wait(ms)` manually using Promise and `setTimeout`.

```javascript
function wait(ms) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      resolve();
    }, ms);
  });
}
```

Exercise question explained:

- "Takes a number ms" means the function accepts the delay in milliseconds.
- "Returns a Promise" means the function gives you a Promise object immediately.
- "Resolves after that many ms" means the Promise completes inside `setTimeout`.
- Then the caller can use `.then(...)` to run code after the delay.

```javascript
wait(1000).then(() => {
  console.log("1 second passed");
});
```

Async/await — cleaner syntax for Promises
`async/await` doesn't replace Promises. It's just cleaner syntax on top of them. Under the hood it's still Promises.

This promise-based pattern:

```javascript
fetch(url)
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then((data) => console.log(data))
  .catch((err) => console.log(err));
```

Is equivalent to this `async/await` pattern:

```javascript
async function getData() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url);
    const data = await res.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(err);
  }
}
```

`await` pauses only that function, not the whole program. Everything else keeps running.

`try/catch` is not optional. If you don't handle errors, they fail silently and you'll spend hours debugging.

Exercise 2 — Fetch users and log name + email
In `day3.js`, the second exercise is `getUsers()`.
It fetches from `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users`, parses JSON, and logs the `name` and `email` for each user.

Key points:

- Use `await fetch(...)` to start the request.
- Use `await res.json()` to parse the response.
- Loop through the array and log only the required fields.
- Wrap it in `try/catch` to handle network or parse errors.

Exercise question explained:

- "Fetches from that URL" means call the public users API.
- "Parses the JSON" means convert the raw response to JavaScript data.
- "Logs only the name and email of each user" means do not print the whole object.
- "Handles errors with try/catch" means catch any failures and print a readable message.

Exercise 3 — Fetch a single user and handle not found
The third exercise is `getUser(id)`.
It fetches one user by ID and handles the "user not found" case.

Important details:

- Valid IDs on this API are usually `1` through `10`.
- If you pass an invalid ID, the API may return an empty object.
- The code checks `if (!data.id)` and prints a "user not found" message.

Exercise question explained:

- "Takes an id" means the function accepts the user ID to request.
- "Fetches from .../users/${id}" means the request uses the ID in the URL.
- "If id is valid, logs the user's name" means print only the `name` field.
- "If you pass id 999... handle that case" means do not rely on success; explicitly detect failure.

Why this exercise matters

- It shows the difference between a successful API response and a missing resource.
- It teaches defensive coding: always check the response before using it.
- It connects real-world async work with UI logic, where missing data is a common case.

Summary of the exercises

1. `wait(ms)` teaches manual Promise creation and delayed resolution.
2. `getUsers()` teaches fetching a list, parsing JSON, and iterating over results.
3. `getUser(id)` teaches fetching a single resource and handling missing data.

Takeaway

- Promises are the foundational async building block.
- `async/await` is easier to read and write.
- Always handle errors with `try/catch`.
- In API work, success and failure both need explicit handling.
